## 📝 0.3 Article Length: Quality-First, Adaptive by City & Data Density

Word count is **NOT** a hardcoded minimum. The core rule across all personas:

> **Write as long as the verified content honestly supports. Never pad. Never stretch. Never invent.**

### Target Ranges (when real data is rich):
| Article Type | Target Words | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Pillar City Guide | 2500–3000 | Full destination; Tier 1 cities always hit this |
| Itinerary (1–3 day) | 2000–2500 | Needs hour-by-hour logistics for each day |
| Top X Things to Do | 1500–2000 | Each entry ~150–200 words of real detail |
| Food & Drink Guide | 1500–2000 | Each venue needs tasting notes + practical info |
| Budget & Practical | 1200–1800 | Hacks and logistics, not fluff |

### Adaptive Rule — Shorter is Fine When:
- The city is **Tier 3** (Invercargill, Gisborne, Whangārei, Palmerston North): fewer real venues, narrower topic. 5 deeply described spots beat 10 thin ones.
- Kiri's Research Dossier returns limited credible data for this specific city/topic combination.
- Expanding further would mean: adding filler transitions, restating points in different words, or inventing detail. **All of these are AI slop.** Reject them.

### Adaptive Rule — Push for Full Length When:
- The city is Tier 1 or Tier 2 with rich, verified Research Dossier data that was not fully used.
- Sections are thin because the Copywriter rushed, not because the data was scarce.

**Bottom line**: A 1100-word Invercargill article that is 100% real, honest, and specific is worth more than a 2000-word Auckland article padded with generic transitions and hollow adjectives.

